UR  - https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2949751526000318
KW  - Midwifery education
KW  - Large language model
KW  - Artificial intelligence
KW  - Critical thinking
KW  - Case-based learning
AB  - Introduction
Developing analytical reasoning, clinical decision-making, and reflective learning are important goals in midwifery education. Large language models may support interactive learning; however, their unstructured use may encourage passive reliance on AI-generated information. This study aims to evaluate a structured “Teacher–Student–AI” interactive model integrating DeepSeek-R1 with case-based learning in undergraduate midwifery education.
Methods
A quasi-experimental, mixed-methods study will be conducted using two consecutive cohorts of undergraduate midwifery students at Sichuan University between September 2026 and January 2028. The comparison cohort will take a conventional course, whereas the intervention cohort will participate in AI-supported case-based learning involving pre-class inquiry, classroom discussion, instructor-guided verification, and post-class reflection. The quantitative outcomes will include critical thinking disposition, self-reported clinical decision-making, caring ability, and academic performance. Outcomes assessed before and after the course will be analyzed using analysis of covariance, adjusting for baseline scores and selected cohort-related variables. Individual interviews will be conducted with consenting students in the intervention cohort to explore their learning experiences. Qualitative data will be analyzed using thematic analysis and integrated with the quantitative findings during interpretation.
Ethics and dissemination
Ethical approval was obtained from the Ethics Committee of the West China Second University Hospital, Sichuan University (No. 2025226). Participation in the study will be voluntary. Only fictionalized or anonymized cases will be used, and students will be prohibited from entering identifiable or sensitive information into the AI platform. The findings will be disseminated through peer-reviewed publications and academic presentations.
Conclusion
This study will provide exploratory evidence of the educational value, implementation challenges, and ethical considerations of a structured AI-supported case-based learning model for midwifery education.
