AB  - Motivated by debates about teachers' roles in (generative) artificial intelligence (Gen) AI-mediated language education and the relative lack of empirical evidence regarding the associated challenges faced by language teachers, this study investigated how learners' (Gen)AI-driven autonomy interacts with mid-career tertiary-level EFL teachers' emotional vulnerabilities and identity tensions. Employing a qualitative design, data were collected through semi-structured interviews and narrative frames. Findings revealed that teachers' emotional vulnerabilities and identity tensions stemmed from three main sources: (a) perceived threats to teachers' conventional respect and credibility images; (b) ethical repercussions of students' enhanced autonomy in (Gen)AI use; and (c) decreased professional agency and fears of potential role replacement. Findings highlight the need for tailored professional development equipping teachers with (Gen)AI literacy to effectively reconcile (Gen)AI affordances with their identity and emotionality, while also urging policymakers to establish clear guidelines defining acceptable (Gen)AI uses by students to safeguard teachers' professional roles in (Gen)AI-enhanced educational contexts.
